    <DataNode elementId="AT3G57520-MONOMER" textLabel="raffinose α-galactosidase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q94A08" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="300" centerY="395" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">SIP2 encodes a raffinose-specific alpha-galactosidase that catalyzes the breakdown of |FRAME: CPD-1099 raffinose| into |FRAME: ALPHA-D-GALACTOSE &amp;alpha;-D-galactose| and |FRAME: SUCROSE sucrose| |CITS: [20739305]|. It does not show any activity with |FRAME: CPD-170 stachyose|, |FRAME: CPD-458 galactinol|, |FRAME: MELIBIOSE melibiose|, or |FRAME: CPD-8065 verbascose| |CITS: [20739305]|. Previous studies suggested that this enzyme would function as a raffinose synthase (see |FRAME: 2.4.1.82-RXN reaction| |CITS: [18502973]|, but this enzyme activity was not detected for recombinant SIP2 |CITS: [20739305]|. Although sucrose is the primary sugar compound transported to sink tissues in the phloem, there is evidence that some raffinose may also be transported in this manner |CITS: [10923710]|. Promoter-based expression assays indicate that SIP2 transcripts are present in sink tissues, such as the phloem in the root steele. In these locations, SIP2 may function to help unload raffinose from the phloem as part of sink metabolism |CITS: [20739305]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT3G57520-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT3G57520.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q94A08" />
      <Property key="Catalyzes" value="RXN-33835, RXN-19113" />
      <Property key="CatalyzesCount" value="2" />
      <Property key="Accession1" value="AT3G57520.1" />
      <Property key="FunctionalAssignmentStatus" value=":PROBABLE-ENZYME" />
      <Property key="Gene" value="AT3G57520" />
      <Property key="InstanceNameTemplate" value="MONOMER-*" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_20739305" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_18502973" />
      <CitationRef elementRef="citation_PUB_10923710" />
    </DataNode>
    <DataNode elementId="AT1G56600-MONOMER" textLabel="galactinol synthase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9FXB2" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="450" centerY="395" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">GolS2 is a galactinol synthase. When, the GolS2 gene was heterologously expressed in &lt;i&gt;E. coli&lt;/i&gt; as a GST-fusion protein, the purified GST-fusion protein was shown to catalyze the synthesis of galactinol from &lt;i&gt;myo&lt;/i&gt;-inositol and UDP-galactose |CITS:[11846875]|. The same activity was observed when GolS2 was overexpressed in Arabidopsis plants |CITS: [18502973]|. GolS2 transcript levels rise in response to methyl viologen, an oxidative damage-inducing agent. Plants over-expressing GolS2 have increased tolerance to salt, chilling, and high-light stress |CITS: [18502973]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G56600-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G56600.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="Q9FXB2" />
